WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.800
From a deleted murderous opening scene and an&nbsp;
alternate ending, to an extended showdown between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.320
Scarlet Witch and Professor X, Multiverse of&nbsp;
Madness was almost an incredibly different movie!
&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.200
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and these&nbsp;
are the deleted scenes and alternate ending you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.840
never got to see in Doctor Strange 2!
Spoilers, of course, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.520
Marvel's Multiverse of Madness&nbsp;
had a pretty chaotic production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.560
from original director Scott Derrickson quitting&nbsp;
over creative differences, to pandemic-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.920
problems, and new director Sam Raimi having to&nbsp;
begin shooting before the script was finalised.
&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.560
In fact, they didn't even have an ending&nbsp;
to the story until halfway through filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.360
and when the movie's release date was flipped&nbsp;
to come after Spider-Man: No Way Home and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.880
WandaVision instead of before, as had been the&nbsp;
plan, numerous other changes had to be made.
&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.520
Of course, these kinds of situations are&nbsp;
part and parcel of moviemaking and can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.400
lead to fantastically enjoyable movies&nbsp;
as well as critically derided ones.
&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.800
But what's the case for Doctor Strange?
Well, according to Raimi, his first cut of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.000
the movie was around two hours and 40 minutes,&nbsp;
over 30 minutes longer than the final version.
&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.160
And we can figure out some of what was&nbsp;
cut from the end movie via missing scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.400
from the trailers and early plot leaks.
Some of these leaks were fairly accurate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.360
when compared to the final film, so what's&nbsp;
missing would be an indicator of changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.320
made due to reshoots as well as cuts made&nbsp;
after feedback from test screenings.
&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.320
One big change made was to the opening&nbsp;
of the movie which, in the leaked plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.960
had Mordo hunting Wanda in "a fiery&nbsp;
burned forest" with "a magical bow".
&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.880
It seems he tracked her down to a cabin&nbsp;
where she was living, perhaps the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.760
we see her in at the end of WandaVision. 
Mordo tried to kill her with magic arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.080
but failed, and instead she ended up&nbsp;
killing him, chopping off his head. 
&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.280
This horror-filled scene set around a cabin&nbsp;
in the woods certainly feels fitting for a Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.480
Raimi film, given his iconic Evil Dead movies.
And it would have followed up on Mordo's story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.120
from the end of the first Doctor Strange movie&nbsp;
where he turned against Stephen and later tracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.960
down Pangborn and took away his powers.
Why are you doing this?
&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.160
Because I see, at long last,&nbsp;
what's wrong with the world.
&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.480
Too many sorcerers.
The cut scene of Mordo hunting Wanda would have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.000
in a way, paid off this cliffhanger from the&nbsp;
first movie, but it seems Marvel decided instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.200
that they want to keep that Mordo alive so he can&nbsp;
return in a future Doctor Strange film and get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.000
proper conclusion to his evil Mordo story&nbsp;
set up at the end of the original film. 
&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.160
Another possibility is that the deleted&nbsp;
scene might have felt too similar to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.800
opening scene of the first Doctor Strange movie&nbsp;
where the villain Kaecilius and his Zealots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.800
decapitated a librarian at the Kamar-Taj.
Plus, not revealing Wanda as a villain right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.120
away in the opening scene keeps alive&nbsp;
a bit of the mystery of who's behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.040
the inter-dimensional monster attacks&nbsp;
on America Chavez a little longer.
&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.360
Another big change to the movie was made&nbsp;
during reshoots when, according to The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.800
Hollywood Reporter, inspired by Spider-Man: No Way&nbsp;
Home, Marvel "decided to have more fun with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.560
multiverse" by adding more cameos and characters.
And it looks like this changed the Illuminati&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.160
line-up in comparison to what was&nbsp;
planned in the original script.
&nbsp;

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:24.119
However, there wasn't any Mister Fantastic&nbsp;
or Black Bolt, so it seems these were later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.119 --> 00:03:28.119
additions and that they replaced Balder&nbsp;
the Brave, who was also originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.119 --> 00:03:32.519
going to be a member of the Illuminati.
In the comics, Balder is an Asgardian and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:36.839
Thor's half-brother, and there were rumours&nbsp;
he was going to be played by Raimi regular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.839 --> 00:03:41.319
Bruce Campbell, who also cameoed in all&nbsp;
three of Raimi's Spider-Man movies.
&nbsp;

00:03:41.319 --> 00:03:46.599
However, Campbell ended up with the part of Pizza&nbsp;
Poppa, the street food vendor who complains about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:51.959
not getting paid and who Strange casts a spell on&nbsp;
to keep hitting himself, in a nod to Campbell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:54.519
famous Evil Dead character.
Groovy.
&nbsp;

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:58.199
It might have been interesting to&nbsp;
see Strange meet Thor's half-brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.199 --> 00:04:03.079
especially after the sorcerer's previous&nbsp;
interaction with Thor and Loki in Ragnarok.
&nbsp;

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:07.719
Wanda's massacre of the Illuminati,&nbsp;
including how she took out Charles Xavier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:11.319
was a huge moment in the movie.
However, in the original plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:16.519
Wanda had an extended confrontation with Professor&nbsp;
X where he floated up to her in his Hover Chair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.319
as we can see in this pre-vis concept art.
His levitating chair would have been a reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:27.079
to Professor X's Hover Chair from the 90s X-Men&nbsp;
animated TV show, and would have fit nicely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.079 --> 00:04:31.079
the theme tune from that series which plays&nbsp;
when Charles first arrives in the film.
&nbsp;

00:04:31.639 --> 00:04:35.319
The ending of Multiverse of Madness&nbsp;
also had numerous significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.319 --> 00:04:40.039
changes compared to the original plot.
The brutal battle at Kamar Taj, where Scarlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.039 --> 00:04:45.399
Witch wiped out almost everyone, came towards the&nbsp;
end rather than at the beginning of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.399 --> 00:04:49.879
and there was also going to be a scene of&nbsp;
Rintrah giving a speech in praise of Wong.
&nbsp;

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:55.319
More significant, though, is that originally after&nbsp;
Wanda decided to destroy the Darkhold castle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.319 --> 00:05:00.679
bury herself under rock, we would have then got&nbsp;
a shot where we actually see her in the rubble.
&nbsp;

00:05:00.679 --> 00:05:06.119
In the final movie, the scene ends instead on a&nbsp;
wide shot of the collapsing castle and mountain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.119 --> 00:05:09.959
and you can see a little burst of&nbsp;
red energy in the crumbling rock.
&nbsp;

00:05:09.959 --> 00:05:14.359
I think this could be read two ways, as&nbsp;
either Wanda making a protective bubble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.359 --> 00:05:18.839
for herself so she can survive or as a&nbsp;
final burst of her magic as she dies.
&nbsp;

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:23.479
I think it's pretty much guaranteed Elizabeth&nbsp;
Olsen will be back, but seeing her in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.399
rubble at the end might have implied&nbsp;
more strongly that she's still alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.399 --> 00:05:32.439
similar perhaps to the end of X-Men Origins:&nbsp;
Wolverine where we see Deadpool, also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.439 --> 00:05:37.639
villain of that story, as his arm crawls out&nbsp;
from under a pile of rubble and his eyes open.
&nbsp;

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:41.639
Of course, if this Wanda is dead,&nbsp;
we could still see Olsen return as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:46.359
a Wanda variant from a different universe.
Would you like to see Scarlet Witch back?
